result.directionVerdict:
Proceed, re-scoped. Forecast-aware promising stays, but the primary instrument becomes a zero-run promise-layer ("display") forecast, scored by exact replay of the existing 170 T3.8 test runs, plus a three-part lateness ledger: dispatch / anticipated traffic / unanticipated traffic. This is the UX and business deliverable. Closed-loop simulation gets only 35 committed runs, about 2.8 GiB, for the two questions replay cannot answer: (1) is Mplus-30's forcing an artefact of naive promises (a robustness test of the thesis's own H2 contrast)? (2) what does putting a forecast into a static-matrix Runner cost in served riders and churn? Up to 17 optional runs (noise robustness, then snow) go only if the live disk gate allows. The rest of the budget is deliberately left unspent, because writing is the bottleneck: the thesis and docs/18/19 still lack T3.8/T3.9.

Rejected:
- the 5-arm closed-loop matrix (over budget, and it mostly measures deterministic consequences of the factor files);
- v1.4 as the test instrument (it needs fresh same-clock naive comparators, runs are about 1.5x larger, and on dev visible60 still went 0.057 -> 0.167);
- a core time-dependent ETA rewrite (7 call sites plus an ADR, too risky in about 5 weeks);
- snow as a stress test (verified: the in-window snow ramp ends at 1.403/1.460, milder than every weekday morning at 1.469-1.656, and the causal forecast there is flat at 0.99-1.01);
- dropping the forecast (in day-disjoint dev, the display layer removed lateness AND churn).

Honest framing that must appear in the thesis: in a uniform citywide-factor world the display result is close to a closed-form consequence of forecast error. It is reported as the measured anticipable share of rush-hour lateness passed through real promise horizons, an upper bound for a time-of-day ratio forecast, with no novelty claim.

decisions.rationale:
Bases are restricted to:
- day-disjoint task-B outcomes;
- published naive test baselines, verified this session;
- input-only factor computations, i.e. r-tables and growth, never forecast outcomes on test cells.

PB1 and PB3 deliberately commit to outcomes that are unfavourable either to the thesis contrast or to the integration option.

decisions.decision:
Proceed with forecast-aware promising in re-scoped form. In priority order:
(i) the zero-run display layer, pads, factorial and ledger on all existing runs;
(ii) 35 committed closed-loop and control runs, plus at most 3 reruns;
(iii) at most 17 optional runs (noise, then snow), gated on live disk;
(iv) the remaining time goes to writing T3.8/T3.9 and this study into the thesis and docs/18/19.

Do not pivot (option C). Do not implement core time-dependent ETAs (option A). Do not adopt the post-hoc 'arrive-by' metric (option B); the display layer reduces churn without redefining 'visible'.

implementationChecks:
Display-layer script with unit tests:
- g ≡ 1 returns p;
- within-bin invariance (same p published at two times in one bin gives the same D);
- the one-sided floor;
- hold after bin 8;
- activity rule (≥ 2 prior non-incident same-kind days; 17/11, 18/11 and 12/11 inactive);
- leakage guard (scenario day never in sources);
- pickup and drop handling;
- closed revisions excluded.
Mutation tests must kill each guard. On task-B the oracle (true-path) stretch must reproduce validate-stretch-2026-10-06.txt (658/660; max |residual| 1.00 s).
